  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 207 AM MDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nFriday into Saturday morning sees little change from one another, \nwith low chances for isolated showers and storms across the high \nterrain. Temperatures continue a slow decline, but are still \nforecast to remain near average for early/mid October. Getting \nfurther through Saturday is when we begin to see a pattern change. A \ndeep upper level low is expected to develop over the Pacific \nNorthwest, while the remnants of Hurricane Rachel begin to shift \ntowards the Desert Southwest after being sheared apart by a previous \nshortwave. Current model and ensemble guidance favor the PNW trough \ndigging down the coast of CA and ejecting the moisture from Rachel \nprimarily into AZ. With this favored advection of moisture, the most \nlikely scenario are isolated to scattered showers developing \nSaturday night across western NM, transitioning to scattered to \nnumerous showers and isolated thunderstorms Sunday evening. \nCurrently, during this timeframe (Saturday-Sunday), rainfall amounts \nlook to remain rather light with amounts up to 0.5\", primarily \nacross western NM. There will likely be an orographic component to\nthe rainfall, so areas close to terrain may see locally heavier \namounts. Additionally, the track of this initial surge of moisture\nmay fluctuate, which would affect the current forecast amounts.\n\nThe second player, with the potential for higher impact, in this \nsetup is another potential tropical system interacting with the low \npressure already in place across southern CA (this potential system \nis currently designated with a 90% chance of cyclone formation from \nthe NHC). While there is lower confidence in what will occur with \nthis second push, both the GFS and ECMWF show a surge of moisture \nadvecting into NM beginning Tuesday afternoon into Wednesday. This \nscenario would bring about more widespread rain and thunderstorms \nacross the region. It is worth mentioning that deterministic models \nhave had poor run-to-run consistency, and ensemble cluster analysis \nis split on whether or not the potential tropical moisture is \nadvected into NM or not. These details will become clearer as we get \ntowards this weekend and into next week. Folks should continue to \ncheck the forecast as this system begins to approach.",
